P=(__import__("pathlib").Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3].as_posix() + "/design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md")
s=open(P,encoding='utf-8').read()
def rep(old,new):
    global s
    assert s.count(old)==1,(s.count(old),old[:70])
    s=s.replace(old,new)
rep(" (TP2, TP7 to TP11, TP13, TP16, TP20 and TP22 do not exist)","")
# step 15
a=s.index('### Step 15.'); b=s.index('### Step 16.')
new15='''### Step 15. Turn the screen, plug its cable into J1 on the screen face, and fold the screen down

- **Join:** DISP1 (`screen`) with its own cable (`screen_tail`) to the board's SCREEN FACE, in this order (layout C of `design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:166`, the design since 2026-11-13). (1) The screen is turned 180 degrees from the way the old text of this file laid it: the ledge where the cable leaves the glass lies at the FINGERTIP end (+X) of the block, not at the hand end (`design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:208`). (2) The cable is bent back under the panel at the fingertip end: a 90 degree fold down at X +13.675, a short straight drop, a 90 degree fold toward the hand end (-X), then a flat run under the panel (`design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:169` to `design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:173`). The inner radius of each fold is 0.30 mm or more; this is the study's start value, because the screen maker gives none (`design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:100`). (3) The cable is plugged into J1 (`screen_connector`) on the screen face at X -4.933, Y -0.003 (`design/ecad/ring/pcb/placement-final.csv:78`). J1's opening faces the fingertip (+X). The cable enters J1 from the fingertip side, lying flat above the board, with its contacts facing UP, away from the board, and contact 1 on the +Y side, the antenna side (ANT1 stands at Y +5.25); the cable end then lies at X -4.833 (`design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:176` to `design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:178`, `design/wiring/wiring-review.md:23`). The lever of J1 is open for this (1.8 mm high open, `design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:59`), the cable goes in, then the lever is closed. While the cable is plugged in, the glass-and-panel unit lies folded away beyond the fingertip end; when the lever is closed the unit is folded down over the board like a book (`design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:211`). (4) NO FOAM FRAME: FOAM1 (`foam`) is not placed as a frame round the board edge, because the cable passes the whole width at the fingertip end. The panel is held from above by the glass and the clear adhesive ADH1 over the whole face (`design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:207`, `design/mfg/specs/cover-glass.md:6`). A spacer may be added only if it leaves X -4.8 to +14.2 open across Y +-6.4; the study does not say whether one is needed, so no spacer is placed until the lead gives its shape (open) (`design/mech/thin/placement.csv:25`, `design/mech/thin/placement.csv:18`).
- **Locate:** J1 by its land pattern on the screen face; the cable's neck, 4.8 mm wide, centred in J1's opening; the screen on the board outline, turned 180 degrees. Clearances of the finished route, each confirmed by the inside model of the taller block: cable to the board 0.370 mm at the stiffened end and 0.400 mm elsewhere, cable to the lower glass 0.500 mm, J1 to the panel 0.050 mm (`design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:184` to `design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:186`).
- **Before:** step 5 passed. The body has the pocket at its fingertip end for the cable's fold, 0.44 mm deep, X 13.725 to 14.165, Y +-6.40 (`design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:206`; confirmed by the inside model of the taller block; step 10 does not list it yet). The software turns the picture 180 degrees (`design/wiring/screen-cable-study.md:209`).
- **Check:** (a) the latch is closed over the whole cable width, seen under a microscope; contact 1 is on the +Y side, checked on the maker's drawing before the lever is closed (`design/datasheets/display_TOH095XVT-01.pdf`, page 5); the cable lies flat in J1 and is not skewed. (b) The 14 signal contacts are the 15 of the cable less contact 3, which has no net and is not connected (`design/ecad/ring/netlist.csv:381`): 1 ELVSS, 2 ELVDD, 4 VCI (VDD_3V0), 5 IOVCC (VDD_1V8), 6 SWIRE, 7 TE (LCD_TE), 8 RESX (LCD_RST), 9 CS (LCD_CS), 10 SCL (LCD_SCK), 11 RS (LCD_DCX), 12 SDA (LCD_MOSI), 13, 14 and 15 GND (`design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:41` to `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:54`). A test pad exists for 4 of them only: contact 5 on TP17 (VDD_1V8) and contacts 13, 14 and 15 on TP18 (GND). The builder measures with an ohmmeter between TP17 and TP18 after the lever is closed: it must not read a short. For the other 10 contacts no pad exists, so their continuity is proven by T-SCREEN at step 18 (open: the lead decides whether the guide needs a better measurement). (c) No short between neighbouring contacts: the only neighbouring contacts with a pad each are none (contact 5 is next to 4 and 6, which have no pad), so this too is proven by T-SCREEN at step 18 and by the ohmmeter reading of (b).
- **After:** nothing irreversible; J1 can be opened until step 17.

'''
s=s[:a]+new15+s[b:]
open(P,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)
